    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Gyeongju was the capital of Silla from the kingdom's traditional founding in 57 BCE until its surrender in 935, and after the unification of the peninsula up to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taedong-river">Taedong River</a> in 668 it was the centre of Korean political and cultural life.</li><li>Under Goryeo and Joseon it lost its national standing, though it kept a place as a regional centre until the provincial capital passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daegu">Daegu</a> in 1601.</li><li>Many of its buildings and sculptures were damaged by invasions and by Neo-Confucian radicals, and the tombs and Buddhist sites that survive are the main record of the Silla city.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The name Sŏrabŏl, by which Gyeongju was known in the Silla period, eventually evolved into a Korean word for a capital city, as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

